    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "A net force, $F$, is applied on an object of mass $m \\text{ kg}$ and causes an acceleration of $a \\text{ m}\\cdot\\text{s}^{-2}$. When the net force, $F$, on the same object is doubled, the resulting acceleration, in $\\text{m}\\cdot\\text{s}^{-2}$, will be ...\n\nA) $a$\nB) $\\frac{1}{2}a$\nC) $2a$\nD) $3a$"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The student needs to identify the relationship between force and acceleration. I will start by asking for the relevant law/formula to ground the student's thinking. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"2 * F / m\") -> 2 * (F/m)]",
      "content": "To solve this, we need to look at the relationship between net force ($F_{net}$), mass ($m$), and acceleration ($a$). Which physical law describes this relationship, and what is its mathematical formula?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "It's Newton's Second Law, $F = ma$. I know the force is doubled, so $2F = ma_{new}$, but I'm not sure how to get the new acceleration in terms of $a$."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The student correctly identified the formula and substituted the doubled force. Now I need to guide them to isolate $a_{new}$ and compare it to the original $a = F/m$.",
      "content": "You are on the right track. If $F = ma$, then the original acceleration is $a = \\frac{F}{m}$. \n\nIf you have the new equation $2F = m \\cdot a_{new}$, can you rearrange it to solve for $a_{new}$ in terms of $F$ and $m$?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "So $a_{new} = \\frac{2F}{m}$. Since $\\frac{F}{m} = a$, then $a_{new} = 2a$. The answer is C."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The student successfully derived the relationship. I will now provide the final answer and the required breakdown. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"2 * (F/m)\") -> 2 * a]",
      "content": "The resulting acceleration is $2a$, which corresponds to option C.\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $F_{net} = ma$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $F_{net}$: The total (net) force acting on the object (measured in Newtons).\n  * $m$: The mass of the object (measured in kilograms).\n  * $a$: The acceleration of the object (measured in $\\text{m}\\cdot\\text{s}^{-2}$).\n* **Why we use it:** Newton's Second Law shows that acceleration is directly proportional to the net force when mass remains constant. If you double the force ($2F$), the acceleration must also double ($2a$) to keep the equation balanced.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** In multiple-choice questions involving proportionality, always write out the original equation and the 'new' equation side-by-side. It makes it much easier to see the factor of change!"
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "step_walkthrough",
    "final_answer": "C",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nF, m, a = sp.symbols('F m a')\n# Original: a = F/m\n# New: a_new = (2*F)/m\n# a_new = 2 * (F/m) = 2*a\nprint('C')",
    "techniques": ["energy_chain", "estimate_first"]
  }
