      "content": "Let's look at how waves behave when they encounter obstacles or gaps! 🌊 Think of water ripples: when waves pass around a barrier or through a doorway, they bend or spread out. This phenomenon is called **diffraction**.\n\nHere is a quick parallel example: Imagine sea waves hitting a breakwater with a small opening. Waves with a large wavelength bend significantly around the edges into the sheltered region, whereas very short ripples tend to cast a sharp shadow.\n\nKeeping this in mind, what property of a wave determines how much it bends around an obstacle or through an opening?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Is it the shorter wavelength? Because smaller things fit through gaps better?"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Address the misconception gently. Diffraction is actually more pronounced when the wavelength is comparable to or larger than the opening. Guide them to compare sound and light wavelengths relative to a door frame.",
      "content": "Waves actually bend (diffract) **more** when their wavelength is comparable to or larger than the size of the obstacle or opening. \n\nConsider the scale: Sound waves in speech have wavelengths ranging from centimeters to a few meters. Visible light waves have tiny wavelengths on the scale of nanometers ($10^{-9}\\text{ m}$). A typical door opening is about $1\\text{ meter}$ wide. \n\nGiven this size, which wave's wavelength is large enough to diffract noticeably around a door frame?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Ah, sound waves have wavelengths in meters, which is close to the door gap size, so sound diffracts! That means sound has a longer wavelength than light. The answer is B."
    },
