      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Confirm correct reasoning and conclusion. Provide final answer, formula/concept breakdown, and exam tip.",
      "content": "Sound waves have much larger wavelengths than light waves, allowing them to undergo significant diffraction around obstacles like doors, while light travels in straight lines.\n\n**Final Answer:** B\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Concept:** Diffraction condition ($\text{Extent of bending} \\propto \\frac{\\lambda}{\\text{width of opening}}$)\n* **What the letters/terms mean:**\n  * $\\lambda$ (lambda): The wavelength of the wave (distance between adjacent wave peaks)\n  * Width of opening: The size of the gap or obstacle the wave passes (e.g., a door frame)\n* **Why we use it:** Because sound waves have longer wavelengths (meters), they easily bend around everyday barriers. Light waves have extremely short wavelengths (nanometers), so they do not diffract around doors and you cannot see around corners.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always remember that diffraction increases as the wavelength ($\\lambda$) approaches or exceeds the size of the aperture/obstacle. Do not confuse diffraction with reflection or refraction!"
    }
  ],
